The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
binder_netlink_report(proc, tcomplete, t, ...);
```
```c
// Inside binder_netlink_report():
pr_debug("%d:%d from %d:%d code %d flags %d pri %d rw %d",
         t->debug_id, ...);
```
```c
// After binder_proc_transaction() returns BR_TRANSACTION_PENDING_FROZEN:
// No bailout; t is still referenced later.
```
Reason:
The transaction pointer `t` is used in `binder_netlink_report()` after the target is frozen. The target can thaw and free `t` at any time, leading to use-after-free when `t` is dereferenced.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
struct binder_netlink_report_data report_data;
report_data.debug_id = t->debug_id;
...
binder_netlink_report(proc, tcomplete, &report_data, ...);
```
```c
/* Added comment in binder_netlink_report(): */
 * Note that t->buffer is not safe to access here, as it may have been
 * released (or not yet allocated).
```
Reason:
Copying the needed transaction fields into a local structure before the lock is released ensures `binder_netlink_report()` accesses stable data, preventing the use‑after‑free. The comment clarifies the limitation.

